\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Notice carefully what these earliest prophecies do 
\f3\i not
\f0\i0  say:
\f2\b0  They do not call the Son "God." They do not say He pre-existed. They do not place Him at creation. They speak of Him as a 
\f0\b Man
\f2\b0  \'97 beloved, only-begotten, born of a woman, of the tribe of Judah, rising from Israel.\
This is the 
\f0\b proper foundation
\f2\b0 . Whatever further glories Scripture reveals about the Son must be built upon 
\f0\b this
\f2\b0  foundation \'97 not in violation of the Father's exclusive deity declared in Isaiah 44-46, but in the manner Scripture itself unfolds.\
Psalm 2 and Psalm 110 (LXX 109) are the most quoted Old Testament texts in the New Testament concerning the Son. They are the keystone of the apostolic preaching about who Jesus is and what office He holds in relation to the One God, the Father.\
