\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Observation \'97 a critical LXX reading\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 The LXX preserves a remarkable line absent from the Hebrew (which the 1599 Geneva follows, reading 
\f1\i "Thy people shall come willingly at the time of assembling thine army in holy beauty: the youth of thy womb shall be as the morning dew"
\f2\i0 ): here the LXX reads, 
\f0\b \uc0\u7952 \u954  \u947 \u945 \u963 \u964 \u961 \u8056 \u962  \u960 \u961 \u8056  \u7953 \u969 \u963 \u966 \u972 \u961 \u959 \u965  \u7952 \u947 \u941 \u957 \u957 \u951 \u963 \u940  \u963 \u949 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "From the womb, before the morning star."
\f2\i0  NETS prints the Greek literally, 
\f1\i "I brought you forth"
\f2\i0 ; Brenton's older English renders it, 
\f1\i "before the morning star I have begotten thee"
\f2\i0  
\f1\i (LXX 1851)
\f2\i0  \'97 
\f0\b \uc0\u7952 \u947 \u941 \u957 \u957 \u951 \u963 \u940  \u963 \u949 
\f2\b0  means 
\f1\i I begot you; I fathered you
\f2\i0 .\
This is the Father speaking again, as in Psalm 2:7. Here a 
\f0\b prior begetting
\f2\b0  is in view \'97 
\f0\b \uc0\u960 \u961 \u8056  \u7953 \u969 \u963 \u966 \u972 \u961 \u959 \u965 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "before the morning star"
\f2\i0  \'97 that is, before the created luminaries of Genesis 1:14-16.\
This is the 
\f0\b second witness
\f2\b0  (alongside Psalm 2:7) to the 
\f1\i begetting
\f2\i0  of the Son \'97 but here the LXX places it 
\f0\b before
\f2\b0  the cosmic order, while Psalm 2:7 places it on the day of resurrection. Scripture itself is presenting 
\f0\b two distinct begettings
\f2\b0  \'97 or more carefully, a foreordained begetting from before time and its historical fulfillment in the resurrection. We will need to examine this carefully later, particularly with Proverbs 8 and Micah 5:2.\
For now, we simply note: 
\f0\b the Son is begotten of the Father
\f2\b0  \'97 never the reverse, never the Father begotten of the Son, and never a "co-eternal" begetting where the Son has no origin in the Father. The Father is 
\f1\i Source
\f2\i0 ; the Son is 
\f1\i Begotten
\f2\i0 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

