\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 The Doctrinal Foundation Now Standing\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 We may now state, by two-or-more witnesses from Scripture itself, what the Son is:\
1. 
\f0\b He is begotten of the Father
\f2\b0  \'97 He has an origin in the Father; the Father has no origin in Him.\
2. 
\f0\b He is appointed, made, and given
\f2\b0  \'97 every title, office, and authority He holds is 
\f0\b conferred upon Him by the Father
\f2\b0 .\
3. 
\f0\b He is distinct from the Father
\f2\b0  \'97 David's Lord, but not the LORD; the Lord's Christ, but not the Lord Himself.\
4. 
\f0\b He is seated at the Father's right hand
\f2\b0  \'97 supreme in honor, but second in order; the Father remains enthroned in the center.\
5. 
\f0\b He is both King (Ps 2) and Priest (Ps 110)
\f2\b0  \'97 combining the offices that were separated in Israel under David and Aaron.\
6. 
\f0\b He is the resurrected One
\f2\b0  \'97 His installation as Son-in-power, Lord, and Christ is bound to His being 
\f0\b raised from the dead
\f2\b0  by the Father.\
This is the apostolic gospel, and it preserves intact the Shema and the declarations of Isaiah 44-46: 
\f0\b the Father is the One God; Jesus is His Christ, His Lord, His Son, His Priest, His King \'97 all by the Father's appointment.
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 A QUESTION ARISING \'97 Was the Son Brought Forth Before the World Was?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Psalm 109:3 LXX has opened the question of the Son's 
\f0\b pre-temporal begetting
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "from the womb, before the morning star."
\f2\i0  Two passages must now be examined to see whether and how Scripture testifies to the Son's existence 
\f1\i before
\f2\i0  His historical birth in Bethlehem and His being declared Son in power at the resurrection: 
\f0\b Proverbs 8
\f2\b0 , where 
\f0\b Wisdom
\f2\b0  speaks in the first person, and 
\f0\b Micah 5:2
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "whose goings forth have been from of old."
\f2\i0 \
We will proceed carefully \'97 letting the text speak, and not retrojecting later "eternal generation" formulations.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 PROVERBS 8 \'97 Wisdom Brought Forth Before the Foundation of the World\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 The chapter is spoken in the 
\f0\b first person
\f2\b0  by 
\f0\b \uc0\u931 \u959 \u966 \u943 \u945 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i Wisdom
\f2\i0  \'97 personified throughout Proverbs 1-9. The question we must ask: 
\f0\b Is this Wisdom the Son? And if so, what does the text say about His origin?
\f2\b0 \
We will not assume the identification; we will let Scripture establish it by two witnesses.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Proverbs 8:22-25 (LXX):\
\pard\pardeftab720\li831\fi-832\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs33\fsmilli16667 \cf0 The Lord created me as the beginning of his ways, for the sake of his works. Before the present age he founded me, in the beginning. Before he made the earth and before he made the depths, before he brought forth the springs of the waters, before the mountains were established and before all the hills, he begets me. \'97 
\f0\b Proverbs 8:22-25, LXX (NETS)
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Observations of decisive importance\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 (1)
\f2\b0  The LXX reads 
\f0\b \uc0\u922 \u973 \u961 \u953 \u959 \u962  \u7956 \u954 \u964 \u953 \u963 \u941 \u957  \u956 \u949 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "The Lord created/founded me."
\f2\i0  The verb is 
\f0\b \uc0\u954 \u964 \u943 \u950 \u969 
\f2\b0 . The Geneva follows the Hebrew 
\f1\i qanah
\f2\i0 , which can mean 
\f1\i possess, acquire, or beget.
\f2\i0  This is the verse over which the great Arian controversy raged in the 4th century \'97 and it is one of the texts the post-Nicene party labored most strenuously to suppress or reinterpret, precisely because the LXX (which Jesus and the Apostles used \'97 per our maxims) reads 
\f0\b \uc0\u7956 \u954 \u964 \u953 \u963 \u941 \u957  \u956 \u949 
\f2\b0 .\

\f0\b Let God be true.
\f2\b0  The LXX says: 
\f0\b The Lord brought-forth/founded Wisdom as the beginning (\uc0\u7936 \u961 \u967 \u942 \u957 ) of His ways, for His works.
\f2\b0 \

\f0\b (2)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u960 \u961 \u8056  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u945 \u7984 \u8182 \u957 \u959 \u962  \u7952 \u952 \u949 \u956 \u949 \u955 \u943 \u969 \u963 \u941 \u957  \u956 \u949  \u7952 \u957  \u7936 \u961 \u967 \u8135 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "Before the age He established me in the beginning."
\f2\i0  Wisdom is founded by the Lord 
\f0\b before time itself
\f2\b0 , before the 
\f0\b \uc0\u945 \u7984 \u974 \u957 
\f2\b0 .\

\f0\b (3)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u960 \u961 \u8056  \u948 \u8050  \u960 \u940 \u957 \u964 \u969 \u957  \u946 \u959 \u965 \u957 \u8182 \u957  \u947 \u949 \u957 \u957 \u8119  \u956 \u949 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "Before all hills He BEGETS me."
