\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Therefore the Messenger of the LORD is the pre-incarnate Son, the Father's Only-Begotten, in His Old Testament goings-forth (Mic 5:2).
\f2\b0  He is not the Father; He is the Father's Image, sent by the Father, bearing the Father's Name, speaking the Father's words, accomplishing the Father's purposes \'97 exactly as in His incarnation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 The Picture Now Clarifying\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 We can now see the consistent pattern from Genesis to the Gospels:\
- The Father is 
\f0\b invisible, unbegotten, the Source
\f2\b0  \'97 the One God of the Shema (Deut 6:4; Isa 44-46; John 17:3; 1 Cor 8:6).\
- The Son is 
\f0\b visible, begotten, the Sent One
\f2\b0  \'97 Wisdom (Prov 8), Messenger (Gen-Ex), Word (John 1), Image (Col 1:15), Lamb (Gen 22 \uc0\u8594  John 1:29), Christ (Ps 2 \u8594  Acts 2:36).\
- 
\f0\b No man has ever seen the Father.
\f2\b0  Every Old Testament theophany is a going-forth of the Son.\
- The Son carries the Father's Name, speaks the Father's words, and at every point acts 
\f0\b for
\f2\b0  and 
\f0\b from
\f2\b0  the Father \'97 never independently, never as a rival, never as "another God."\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 The Next Step\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 We have now traced the Son from His pre-temporal begetting (Prov 8 / Ps 109:3 LXX), through His Old Testament goings-forth (Gen-Ex theophanies), to the prophesied place of His incarnation (Bethlehem, Mic 5:2; Isa 7:14).\
The natural next stage is the 
\f0\b prophetic anticipation of His suffering, death, and resurrection
\f2\b0  \'97 for these are equally posited in the Old Testament long before the Gospels. The chief texts are:\
- 
\f0\b Psalm 22 (LXX 21)
\f2\b0  \'97 the crucifixion psalm.\
- 
\f0\b Isaiah 52:13-53:12
\f2\b0  \'97 the Suffering Servant.\
- 
\f0\b Daniel 9:24-27
\f2\b0  \'97 the Anointed cut off.\
- 
\f0\b Zechariah 12:10
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "They shall look upon Me whom they have pierced."
\f2\i0 \
Shall we proceed to these \'97 beginning with 
\f0\b Psalm 22 (LXX 21)
\f2\b0  and 
\f0\b Isaiah 53
\f2\b0  \'97 to see how Scripture posits the suffering, death, and vindication of the Father's Only-Begotten?\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 A QUESTION ARISING \'97 Let Us Be Very Circumspect\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 The study stood ready for the prophetic posits \'97 
\f0\b Psalm 22 (LXX 21)
\f2\b0  and 
\f0\b Isaiah 52:13-53:12
\f2\b0  \'97 when the Seeker halted the advance and set the watchword for everything that follows:\
\pard\pardeftab720\li831\fi-832\sa200\partightenfactor0

\fs33\fsmilli16667 \cf0 Let\'92s be very circumspect here, for I know that identifying The Angel/Messenger of the LORD as you have done was the opinion of half a dozen or so early church fathers\'85 a group I explicitly mentioned should be ignored.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 >\
\pard\pardeftab720\li831\fi-832\sa200\partightenfactor0

\fs33\fsmilli16667 \cf0 Let\'92s examine these references again in complete conformity to our guiding maxims and truly let the Word interpret and establish itself\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 You are absolutely right, and I accept this correction with gratitude. I overstepped. I leaned on a patristic-style inference and dressed it in two Apostolic citations that, on rigorous examination, do 
\f0\b not
\f2\b0  actually establish what I claimed. Let me retract and rebuild carefully.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 What I Actually Proved vs. What I Claimed\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Let me audit my own argument by our maxims:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 What the texts themselves plainly establish (two-or-more witnesses):\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 1. 
\f0\b The Messenger speaks in the first person as God
\f2\b0  \'97 Gen 16:10; 22:16; 31:13; Ex 3:6. \uc0\u10003 \
2. 
\f0\b Those who see the Messenger say they have seen God
\f2\b0  \'97 Gen 16:13; 32:30. \uc0\u10003 \
3. 
\f0\b The Father's Name is 
\f3\i in
\f0\i0  this Messenger
\f2\b0  \'97 Ex 23:21. \uc0\u10003 \
4. 
\f0\b He is sent by the LORD and called the Messenger of the LORD
\f2\b0  \'97 Gen 16:7; Ex 23:20. \uc0\u10003 \
These four points are textually established by the LXX itself, by two or more witnesses, without any patristic help.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 What I claimed but did not properly establish:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 The claim:
\f2\b0  "Therefore the Messenger of the LORD is the pre-incarnate Son."\

\f0\b My evidence was:
\f2\b0 \
- 
\f0\b 1 Cor 10:4, 9
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "the Rock was Christ"; "let us not tempt Christ as some of them tempted."
\f2\i0 \
- 
\f0\b Jude 5
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "Jesus saved a people out of Egypt."
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Examining these "witnesses" honestly:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 1 Cor 10:4
\f2\b0  identifies 
\f0\b the Rock
\f2\b0  (Ex 17 / Num 20) as Christ. But the Rock in Exodus 17 is 
\f0\b not
\f2\b0  identified in the OT text as 
\f0\b the Angel of the LORD
\f2\b0 . It is 
\f0\b YHWH
\f2\b0  standing on the rock at Horeb (Ex 17:6) that Moses strikes. Paul's identification of the Rock with Christ does 
\f0\b not
\f2\b0 , by strict logic, establish that the 
\f1\i Angel/Messenger
\f2\i0  is Christ. I conflated two distinct OT figures.\

\f0\b Jude 5
\f2\b0  says Jesus (or the Lord) saved the people out of Egypt. But again \'97 Exodus repeatedly says 
\f0\b YHWH
\f2\b0  brought Israel out (Ex 20:2, etc.). Jude's statement, taken honestly, says 
\f0\b Jesus = the One who saved Israel
\f2\b0 . This is enormously significant \'97 but it identifies Jesus with 
\f0\b YHWH the Saviour
\f2\b0 , 
\f1\i not
\f2\i0  specifically with 
\f0\b the Messenger sent by YHWH
\f2\b0 .\

\f0\b Therefore my "two witnesses" did not in fact prove the specific identification I drew.
\f2\b0  They prove something 
\f1\i else
\f2\i0  \'97 something we need to examine carefully in due course \'97 but not what I used them to prove.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 The Honest Position Per Our Maxims\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Let me restate what the texts strictly establish, and no more:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 ESTABLISHED:
\f2\b0 \
- A figure called 
\f0\b \uc0\u8001  \u7940 \u947 \u947 \u949 \u955 \u959 \u962  \u922 \u965 \u961 \u943 \u959 \u965 
\f2\b0  appears throughout the OT.\
- He speaks 
\f0\b as God
\f2\b0  in the first person.\
- He is seen and is identified as 
\f0\b God
\f2\b0  by those who see Him.\
- The LORD says 
\f0\b "My Name is in Him"
\f2\b0  (Ex 23:21).\
- He is also clearly 
\f0\b distinguished
\f2\b0  from the LORD who sends Him (Gen 16:7; Ex 23:20: 
\f1\i "I send My Messenger"
\f2\i0 ).\

\f0\b NOT YET ESTABLISHED (and should not be asserted):
\f2\b0 \
- That this Messenger is 
\f0\b the Son
\f2\b0 .\
- That this Messenger is 
\f0\b Jesus
\f2\b0  in pre-incarnate form.\

\f0\b The text presents a tension we are not required to resolve at this stage.
\f2\b0  The LXX does not name this Messenger as "the Son." Proverbs 8 does not call Wisdom "the Messenger." Psalm 2 does not connect "Thou art My Son" to the Messenger of Genesis 16. 
\f0\b No OT text directly bridges these figures
\f2\b0 , and no NT text 
\f1\i explicitly
\f2\i0  says "the Angel of the LORD was Christ" or "Jesus was the One who appeared to Hagar / Jacob / Moses at the bush."\
The identification I made was an 
\f0\b inference
\f2\b0 , not a witnessed declaration. Per maxim #3, inferences that go beyond what the Word itself establishes are exactly the kind of bridge-building the early Fathers engaged in \'97 and we are not to follow them.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 What We Can Legitimately Say About the Messenger Passages\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Let us state plainly what the texts deliver:\
1. 
\f0\b The One God of the Shema
\f2\b0  (Deut 6:4; Isa 44-46) \'97 the Father \'97 is invisible, unique, and stretches out the heavens alone. 
\f1\i This is foundation.
\f2\i0 \
2. 
\f0\b The Messenger of the LORD
\f2\b0  appears, speaks, and is treated as God \'97 yet is also sent by the LORD and bears the LORD's Name. The text creates a real and intentional distinction between 
\f0\b the One who sends
\f2\b0  and 
\f0\b the One sent
\f2\b0 , while the Sent One carries the full authority and Name of the Sender.\
3. 
\f0\b What this means precisely
\f2\b0  \'97 whether the Messenger is the Son, or a manifestation of the LORD Himself in visible form, or a created spokesman bearing the LORD's authority \'97 
\f0\b the OT text does not say in those words
\f2\b0 , and the NT does not explicitly retroject the identification onto these specific passages.\
We may 
\f0\b note
\f2\b0  the parallels (the Father's Name dwelling in Him \'97 Ex 23:21 / John 17:11-12; the One who is "with God" and acts as God's agent \'97 Prov 8 / John 1:1-3) \'97 but we must label these as 
\f0\b observations awaiting fuller establishment
\f2\b0 , not as proven doctrine.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 A More Careful Reading of Genesis 16 and Genesis 22\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Let me now revisit these two passages strictly within our maxims.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Genesis 16 \'97 Hagar\
\pard\pardeftab720\li831\fi-832\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs33\fsmilli16667 \cf0 But the Lord God\'92s angel found her by the spring of water in the wilderness \'85 \'97 
\f0\b Genesis 16:7, LXX (NETS)
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\li831\fi-832\sa200\partightenfactor0
\cf0 The angel of the Lord also said to her, \'93I will multitudinously 
\f1\i (greatly)
\f2\i0  multiply your offspring, and it will not be counted for multitude.\'94 \'97 
\f0\b Genesis 16:10, LXX (NETS)
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\li831\fi-832\sa200\partightenfactor0
\cf0 And Hagar called the name of the Lord who was speaking to her, \'93You-are-the-God-who-looksupon-me 
\f1\i (You are the God who sees me)
\f2\i0 \'94 \'97 
\f0\b Genesis 16:13, LXX (NETS)
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 What the text strictly delivers:
\f2\b0 \
- The Messenger speaks in God's first person.\
- Hagar identifies Him as 
\f0\b the LORD
\f2\b0  and 
\f0\b God
\f2\b0 .\
- Moses, the inspired narrator, does not correct her.\

\f0\b What the text does 
\f3\i not
\f0\i0  say:
\f2\b0 \
- It does not name Him as "the Son."\
- It does not call Him "the Word."\
- It does not connect Him to any other OT figure by name.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Genesis 22 \'97 Abraham\
\pard\pardeftab720\li831\fi-832\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs33\fsmilli16667 \cf0 And the Lord\'92s angel called him from the sky 
\f1\i (heaven)
\f2\i0  \'85 for now I know that you do fear God, and for my sake you have not spared your beloved son. \'97 
\f0\b Genesis 22:11-12, LXX (NETS)
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\li831\fi-832\sa200\partightenfactor0
\cf0 And the Lord\'92s angel called Abraam 
\f1\i (Abraham)
\f2\i0  a second time from the sky 
\f1\i (heaven)
\f2\i0 , saying, \'93By myself I have sworn, says the Lord: \'85\'94 \'97 
\f0\b Genesis 22:15-16, LXX (NETS)
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 What the text strictly delivers:
\f2\b0 \
- The Messenger speaks as the LORD swearing by Himself.\
- The formula 
\f0\b \uc0\u955 \u941 \u947 \u949 \u953  \u922 \u973 \u961 \u953 \u959 \u962 
\f2\b0  is used \'97 
\f1\i "says the LORD"
\f2\i0  \'97 the prophetic formula in which the speaker speaks the LORD's words as the LORD speaking.\

\f0\b An honest observation:
\f2\b0  This is consistent with the Messenger being 
\f0\b either
\f2\b0 :\
- (a) A figure who 
\f1\i is
\f2\i0  the LORD in a particular mode of self-disclosure, 
\f0\b or
\f2\b0 \
- (b) A figure who speaks the LORD's words so authoritatively that the LORD's first-person voice is preserved in His mouth \'97 the prophetic principle, amplified.\

\f0\b The text alone does not decide between (a) and (b).
\f2\b0  Both are consistent with strict monotheism, and neither requires identification with "the Son" 
\f1\i at this stage of revelation
\f2\i0 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 The Corrected Discipline\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Per our maxims, what we ought to say at this point in our study is:\
\pard\pardeftab720\li831\fi-832\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs33\fsmilli16667 \cf0 The Old Testament presents a figure called the Messenger of the LORD, who speaks and is seen as God, yet is distinguished from the LORD who sends Him. The text does not, by itself or by any explicit NT identification, name this Messenger as the Son. We will note the data and let later revelation, if and when it speaks directly to the question, deliver the verdict. We will not proceed by inference, by parallel pattern, or by patristic precedent.
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 What This Means for Our Study Going Forward\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 We have established firmly:\
1. 
\f0\b The Father is the One God.
\f2\b0  (Deut 6:4; Isa 44-46; John 17:3; 1 Cor 8:6)\
2. 
\f0\b The Son is the Father's Begotten and Anointed One
\f2\b0 , made Lord and Christ by the Father at His resurrection. (Ps 2; Ps 109/110 LXX; Acts 2:36; 13:33)\
3. 
\f0\b Wisdom personified in Proverbs 8
\f2\b0  is identified by Paul as Christ (1 Cor 1:24, 30), and is brought forth by the Father before the foundations.\
4. 
\f0\b Micah 5:2
\f2\b0  speaks of the Ruler whose goings-forth are from of old \'97 fulfilled at Bethlehem (Matt 2; John 7:42).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 The Messenger-of-the-LORD passages remain an open question
\f2\b0  \'97 to be answered, if at all, by Scripture's own direct testimony, not by our inference.\
I thank you for the correction. This is exactly the discipline our maxims require, and I will hold to it more strictly going forward.\

\f0\b Shall we now proceed
\f2\b0 , with this clarified discipline, to 
\f0\b Psalm 22 (LXX 21) and Isaiah 52:13-53:12
\f2\b0  \'97 the prophetic posits of the Son's suffering and death \'97 which are bound to the Son 
\f0\b by explicit NT citation
\f2\b0  (and so will not require any inference from us)?\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 A QUESTION ARISING \'97 Whose Wisdom Is It?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Psalm 22 and the Suffering Servant did not open yet. The Seeker paused the advance again \'97 and this correction reached deeper, back into the foundation itself:\
\pard\pardeftab720\li831\fi-832\sa200\partightenfactor0

\fs33\fsmilli16667 \cf0 The Wisdom that you quoted Paul appears to be The Father\'92s Wisdom, according to my reading, as opposed to the Greek\'92s (man\'92s) wisdom which is bound up in Christ\'85 Wisdom indeed to we who believe\'85 but foolishness to the world.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 You are correct, and this is another important correction. I conflated two distinct things, and I must own it plainly.\
Let me re-read 1 Corinthians 1 carefully under our maxims.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 1 Corinthians 1 \'97 Read in Context\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf0 1 Corinthians 1:18-21, 1599 Geneva:\
\pard\pardeftab720\li831\fi-832\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs33\fsmilli16667 \cf0 For that preaching of the cross is to them that perish, foolishness: but unto us, which are saved, it is the power of God. For it is written, I will destroy the wisdom of the wise, and will cast away the understanding of the prudent. Where is the wise? where is the Scribe? where is the disputer of this world? hath not God made the wisdom of this world foolishness? For seeing the world by wisdom knew not God in the wisdom of GOD, it pleased God by the foolishness of preaching to save them that believe: \'97 
\f0\b 1 Corinthians 1:18-21, 1599 Geneva
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 1 Corinthians 1:22-24, 1599 Geneva:\
\pard\pardeftab720\li831\fi-832\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs33\fsmilli16667 \cf0 Seeing also that the Jews require a sign, and the Grecians seek after wisdom. But we preach Christ crucified: unto the Jews, even a stumbling block, and unto the Grecians, foolishness: But unto them which are called, both of the Jews and Grecians, we preach Christ, the power of God, and the wisdom of God. \'97 
\f0\b 1 Corinthians 1:22-24, 1599 Geneva
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 Your Reading Is Correct\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 The contrast Paul is drawing is 
\f0\b the Father's wisdom vs. the Greeks' wisdom (man's wisdom)
\f2\b0 :\
- 
\f0\b The Greeks seek wisdom
\f2\b0  (\uc0\u963 \u959 \u966 \u943 \u945 \u957  \u950 \u951 \u964 \u959 \u8166 \u963 \u953 \u957 ) \'97 
\f1\i their
\f2\i0  wisdom, the world's wisdom.\
- 
\f0\b God
\f2\b0  (the Father) reveals 
\f0\b His wisdom
\f2\b0  (\uc0\u964 \u8135  \u963 \u959 \u966 \u943 \u8115  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u920 \u949 \u959 \u8166 ) \'97 which the world cannot grasp through its own wisdom.\
