\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0
\cf0 #### 
\f0\b Phrase 3: \uc0\u954 \u945 \u8054  \u920 \u949 \u8056 \u962  \u7974 \u957  \u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "and the Word was God."
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 What the text strictly says \'97 observe carefully:
\f2\b0 \
- 
\f0\b \uc0\u920 \u949 \u8056 \u962 
\f2\b0  here has 
\f0\b no article
\f2\b0  \'97 it is 
\f1\i anarthrous
\f2\i0  (without 
\f0\b \uc0\u8001 
\f2\b0 ).\
- The subject is 
\f0\b \uc0\u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 
\f2\b0  (with article); the predicate is 
\f0\b \uc0\u920 \u949 \u8056 \u962 
\f2\b0  (no article).\

\f0\b The Greek grammar matters:
\f2\b0 \
- In v. 1b, the Word is 
\f0\b \uc0\u960 \u961 \u8056 \u962  \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u957 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i with
\f2\i0  
\f0\b the
\f2\b0  
\f0\b God
\f2\b0  (the Father, with article).\
- In v. 1c, the Word is 
\f0\b \uc0\u920 \u949 \u8056 \u962 
\f2\b0  \'97 anarthrous, predicate nominative.\
The grammatical distinction is 
\f1\i deliberate
\f2\i0  on John's part. In v. 1b, 
\f0\b \uc0\u8001  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 
\f2\b0  = the Father (identified Person). In v. 1c, 
\f0\b \uc0\u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 
\f2\b0  (anarthrous) is being used qualitatively \'97 describing the 
\f1\i nature
\f2\i0 , 
\f1\i character
\f2\i0 , or 
\f1\i category
\f2\i0  of the Word, not identifying Him as 
\f0\b the God
\f2\b0  of v. 1b.\

\f0\b Compare:
\f2\b0  If John had written 
\f0\b \uc0\u954 \u945 \u8054  \u8001  \u920 \u949 \u8056 \u962  \u7974 \u957  \u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 
\f2\b0  (with article), it would mean 
\f1\i "and the Word was [identically] the God [with whom He was]"
\f2\i0  \'97 a flat contradiction with v. 1b. John deliberately avoided that. He wrote 
\f0\b \uc0\u920 \u949 \u8056 \u962  \u7974 \u957  \u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 
\f2\b0  to say something different \'97 what exactly, we must let Scripture interpret.\

\f0\b Honest observation:
\f2\b0  The text says the Word 
\f0\b was God
\f2\b0  in some sense, but the 
\f1\i grammar itself
\f2\i0  prevents collapsing the Word into the Person of the Father (who is 
\f0\b \uc0\u8001  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 
\f2\b0  of v. 1b).\
