\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Notice \'97 the Geneva translators rendered the pronouns as "it"
\f2\b0  \'97 treating 
\f0\b \uc0\u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 
\f2\b0  as the Word/utterance/expression, 
\f0\b not yet
\f2\b0  as a personal pronoun "He." This is honest to the Greek at this stage. The Greek pronoun 
\f0\b \uc0\u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8166 
\f2\b0  is grammatically masculine (matching 
\f0\b \uc0\u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 
\f2\b0 , which is masculine in gender), but Greek grammatical masculinity does not by itself prove personal masculinity. (E.g., 
\f0\b \uc0\u8001  \u957 \u972 \u956 \u959 \u962 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i the law
\f2\i0  \'97 is grammatically masculine; we still say "it.")\

\f0\b The Geneva's choice to translate "it" reflects an honest reading of the Greek up to this point
\f2\b0 : the Word may be a personification of God's creative utterance/plan, not yet established as a distinct personal being.\
