\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 HONEST ASSESSMENT OF JOHN 1\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf0 What John 1 
\f3\i strictly
\f0\i0  establishes by its own text + cross-witnesses:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 1. There is 
\f0\b the Word
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f0\b \uc0\u8001  \u923 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u962 
\f2\b0  \'97 present at the beginning (echoing Gen 1:1).\
2. The Word was 
\f0\b with God [the Father]
\f2\b0  and was 
\f0\b God
\f2\b0  (anarthrous \'97 
\f1\i qualitatively divine
\f2\i0 , not identically the Person of the Father).\
3. 
\f0\b All things came to be through the Word.
\f2\b0  (Cross-witness: Ps 33:6 LXX \'97 the Father creates by His Word.)\
4. 
\f0\b The Word became flesh
\f2\b0  and was beheld as the Only-Begotten from the Father \'97 i.e., the Word is identified with Jesus the Son in His incarnation.\
5. 
\f0\b No one has ever seen the Father
\f2\b0  \'97 confirmed by John 1:18. The Father remains the One Unseen God.\
6. 
\f0\b The Son declares/exegetes the Father.
\f2\b0  He is the Revealer.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 What remains 
\f3\i indeterminate
\f0\i0  from the strict text:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 - 
\f0\b Whether the Word was a personal pre-existent Being
\f2\b0  
\f1\i as the Son
\f2\i0  before becoming flesh, 
\f0\b or whether the Word was the Father's own creative utterance/plan/expression
\f2\b0  that took personal form 
\f1\i as the Son
\f2\i0  in the incarnation.\
The Greek text \'97 strictly read \'97 does not force the choice. Both readings are consistent with:\
- Isa 44:24 (the Father creates alone \'97 by His Word, which is His own).\
- The Shema (one God \'97 the Father).\
- John 17:3 (the Father is the only true God).\
- Acts 2:36, 13:33, Rom 1:4 (the Son was 
\f1\i made
\f2\i0  / 
\f1\i declared
\f2\i0  / 
\f1\i begotten
\f2\i0  as Lord, Christ, and Son in power at His resurrection).\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 A Significant Implication\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Both Readings A and B 
\f1\i preserve
\f2\i0  our foundation:\
- The Father remains the One God.\
- The Word/Son comes forth from the Father.\
- The Father creates by His Word (whether personally pre-existent or as His expressed creative act).\
- The Word 
\f1\i is
\f2\i0  the Son in His incarnation \'97 manifested as Jesus.\
What we should 
\f0\b not
\f2\b0  do is import the post-Nicene formulation of "co-eternal co-equal Persons of one substance" into John 1. The text does not say that. It says the Word was 
\f0\b with
\f2\b0  the Father and was 
\f0\b \uc0\u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 
\f2\b0  qualitatively \'97 divine in character \'97 which is something less specific than later creedal language.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 What This Means for Our Study\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 We can honestly say:\
1. 
\f0\b John 1 identifies the incarnate Jesus as "the Word made flesh"
\f2\b0  \'97 established by v. 14.\
2. 
\f0\b The Word was present and active at the beginning
\f2\b0  \'97 through whom all things came to be.\
3. 
\f0\b The Word was with God (the Father)
\f2\b0  and was 
\f0\b qualitatively divine.
\f2\b0 \
4. 
\f0\b The Word and the Father are distinguished
\f2\b0  \'97 the Word is "with" God, not identical to "the God" with whom He was.\
What we 
\f0\b cannot
\f2\b0  firmly say without going beyond the text:\
- That the Word was a fully distinct personal Being from eternity in the Nicene sense.\
- That the Word is precisely "the Angel of the LORD" or "Wisdom" of Proverbs 8 (no NT citation binds these).\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
