\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Observations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 (1)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u7952 \u957  \u956 \u959 \u961 \u966 \u8135  \u920 \u949 \u959 \u8166  \u8017 \u960 \u940 \u961 \u967 \u969 \u957 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "existing in the form of God."
\f2\i0  
\f0\b \uc0\u956 \u959 \u961 \u966 \u942 
\f2\b0  = form, outward expression of essential nature. 
\f0\b \uc0\u8017 \u960 \u940 \u961 \u967 \u969 \u957 
\f2\b0  = continuously existing, subsisting.\
This is a significant witness. Paul says Christ 
\f0\b was existing in the form of God
\f2\b0  
\f1\i before
\f2\i0  taking the form of a servant.\

\f0\b (2)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u959 \u8016 \u967  \u7937 \u961 \u960 \u945 \u947 \u956 \u8056 \u957  \u7969 \u947 \u942 \u963 \u945 \u964 \u959  \u964 \u8056  \u949 \u7990 \u957 \u945 \u953  \u7988 \u963 \u945  \u920 \u949 \u8183 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "did not regard being equal with God a thing to be grasped/seized."
\f2\i0  The phrase is contested in translation. Reading carefully:\
- 
\f0\b \uc0\u7937 \u961 \u960 \u945 \u947 \u956 \u972 \u962 
\f2\b0  = a thing seized, plunder, something to be grasped or held onto.\
- 
\f0\b \uc0\u964 \u8056  \u949 \u7990 \u957 \u945 \u953  \u7988 \u963 \u945  \u920 \u949 \u8183 
\f2\b0  = 
\f1\i "the being equal with God."
\f2\i0 \
Two main readings:\
- 
\f0\b Reading 1:
\f2\b0  He already was equal with God but did not consider it something to cling to (selfless renunciation).\
- 
\f0\b Reading 2:
\f2\b0  He did not consider equality with God something to be seized/grasped at (unlike Adam, who grasped at it in Eden).\
The Geneva's 
\f1\i "thought it no robbery to be equal with God"
\f2\i0  leans toward reading 1, but the Greek is genuinely contested.\

\f0\b (3)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u7953 \u945 \u965 \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u7952 \u954 \u941 \u957 \u969 \u963 \u949 \u957 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "He emptied Himself."
\f2\i0  This implies a prior state from which He emptied.\

\f0\b (4)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u956 \u959 \u961 \u966 \u8052 \u957  \u948 \u959 \u973 \u955 \u959 \u965  \u955 \u945 \u946 \u974 \u957 , \u7952 \u957  \u8001 \u956 \u959 \u953 \u974 \u956 \u945 \u964 \u953  \u7936 \u957 \u952 \u961 \u974 \u960 \u969 \u957  \u947 \u949 \u957 \u972 \u956 \u949 \u957 \u959 \u962 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "taking the form of a servant, becoming in the likeness of men."
\f2\i0  The 
\f1\i taking
\f2\i0  and 
\f1\i becoming
\f2\i0  imply a transition from a prior state.\

\f0\b (5)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u948 \u953 \u8056  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u8001  \u920 \u949 \u8056 \u962  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u8017 \u960 \u949 \u961 \u973 \u968 \u969 \u963 \u949 \u957 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "Therefore God has highly exalted Him."
\f2\i0  The Father (
\f0\b \uc0\u8001  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u962 
\f2\b0 ) is the One who exalts. The Son is the One exalted.\

\f0\b (6)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u7952 \u967 \u945 \u961 \u943 \u963 \u945 \u964 \u959  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8183  \u964 \u8056  \u8004 \u957 \u959 \u956 \u945 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "He [the Father] graciously gave Him the Name."
\f2\i0  The Name above every name is 
\f0\b given
\f2\b0  to the Son by the Father \'97 not innately possessed.\

\f0\b (7)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u949 \u7984 \u962  \u948 \u972 \u958 \u945 \u957  \u920 \u949 \u959 \u8166  \u928 \u945 \u964 \u961 \u972 \u962 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "to the glory of God the Father."
\f2\i0  Every confession of Jesus as Lord is 
\f0\b to the Father's glory.
\f2\b0  The Father remains the ultimate object.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 What Phil 2:5-11 establishes (honestly):\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 - Christ existed 
\f0\b in the form of God
\f2\b0  before His incarnation.\
- He 
\f0\b emptied Himself
\f2\b0  and 
\f0\b took
\f2\b0  the form of a servant \'97 a real transition.\
- He was 
\f0\b exalted by the Father
\f2\b0  after His obedience to death.\
- The 
\f0\b Name
\f2\b0  was 
\f0\b given
\f2\b0  to Him by the Father.\
- Every confession of His Lordship is 
\f0\b to the Father's glory
\f2\b0  \'97 the Father remains supreme.\
This is the 
\f0\b strongest single NT witness to a pre-incarnate state of the Son
\f2\b0  in some form of divine glory. The Father remains supreme; the Son acts in dependence and is exalted by the Father.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 WITNESS B: Colossians 1:13-20\
\pard\pardeftab720\li831\fi-832\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs33\fsmilli16667 \cf0 Who hath delivered us from the power of darkness, and hath translated us into the kingdom of his dear Son, In whom we have redemption through his blood, that is, the forgiveness of sins, Who is the image of the invisible God, the first begotten of every creature. For by him were all things created, which are in heaven, and which are in earth, things visible and invisible: whether they be Thrones or Dominions, or Principalities, or Powers, all things were created by him, and for him, And he is before all things, and in him all things consist. And he is the head of the body of the Church: he is the beginning, and the first begotten of the dead, that in all things he might have the preeminence. For it pleased the Father, that in him should all fulness dwell. And through peace made by that blood of that his cross, to reconcile to himself through him, through him, I say, all things, both which are in earth, and which are in heaven. \'97 
\f0\b Colossians 1:13-20, 1599 Geneva
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Observations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 (1)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u949 \u7984 \u954 \u8060 \u957  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u920 \u949 \u959 \u8166  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u7936 \u959 \u961 \u940 \u964 \u959 \u965 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "the image of the invisible God."
\f2\i0  The Son is the 
\f0\b Image
\f2\b0  of the unseen Father. (Connects with John 1:18 \'97 
\f1\i "No one has seen God; the Only-Begotten Son has declared Him."
\f2\i0 )\

\f0\b (2)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u960 \u961 \u969 \u964 \u972 \u964 \u959 \u954 \u959 \u962  \u960 \u940 \u963 \u951 \u962  \u954 \u964 \u943 \u963 \u949 \u969 \u962 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "firstborn of all creation."
\f2\i0  This phrase is contested:\
- 
\f0\b Reading 1:
\f2\b0  "Firstborn [over] all creation" \'97 preeminent over creation.\
- 
\f0\b Reading 2:
\f2\b0  "Firstborn [of/within] all creation" \'97 first in the order of created beings.\
The Greek genitive 
\f0\b \uc0\u960 \u940 \u963 \u951 \u962  \u954 \u964 \u943 \u963 \u949 \u969 \u962 
\f2\b0  admits both. Verse 16 immediately clarifies: 
\f0\b \uc0\u8005 \u964 \u953  \u7952 \u957  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8183  \u7952 \u954 \u964 \u943 \u963 \u952 \u951  \u964 \u8048  \u960 \u940 \u957 \u964 \u945 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "because in Him were all things created."
\f2\i0  This 
\f0\b \uc0\u8005 \u964 \u953 
\f2\b0  (because) gives the reason for the firstborn-title: He is firstborn 
\f1\i because
\f2\i0  all things were created in/through/for Him.\
If He is the one through/for whom all things were created, He cannot be a created thing within all things \'97 so Reading 1 is favored by context.\

\f0\b (3)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u7952 \u957  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8183  \u7952 \u954 \u964 \u943 \u963 \u952 \u951  \u964 \u8048  \u960 \u940 \u957 \u964 \u945 ... \u964 \u8048  \u960 \u940 \u957 \u964 \u945  \u948 \u953 \u8125  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u949 \u7984 \u962  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u7956 \u954 \u964 \u953 \u963 \u964 \u945 \u953 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "In Him all things were created... all things have been created through Him and unto Him."
\f2\i0 \
Three prepositions, three relations to creation:\
- 
\f0\b \uc0\u7952 \u957  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8183 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i in Him
\f2\i0  (the sphere)\
- 
\f0\b \uc0\u948 \u953 \u8125  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8166 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i through Him
\f2\i0  (the agency)\
- 
\f0\b \uc0\u949 \u7984 \u962  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u972 \u957 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i unto Him
\f2\i0  (the goal)\

\f0\b (4)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u954 \u945 \u8054  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u972 \u962  \u7952 \u963 \u964 \u953 \u957  \u960 \u961 \u8056  \u960 \u940 \u957 \u964 \u969 \u957 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "and He is before all things."
\f2\i0  The Son is 
\f0\b prior to all things.
\f2\b0 \

\f0\b (5)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u954 \u945 \u8054  \u964 \u8048  \u960 \u940 \u957 \u964 \u945  \u7952 \u957  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8183  \u963 \u965 \u957 \u941 \u963 \u964 \u951 \u954 \u949 \u957 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "and in Him all things hold together."
\f2\i0  The Son sustains creation.\

\f0\b (6)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u8005 \u964 \u953  \u7952 \u957  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u8183  \u949 \u8016 \u948 \u972 \u954 \u951 \u963 \u949 \u957  \u960 \u8118 \u957  \u964 \u8056  \u960 \u955 \u942 \u961 \u969 \u956 \u945  \u954 \u945 \u964 \u959 \u953 \u954 \u8134 \u963 \u945 \u953 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "because in Him it pleased [the Father] that all fullness should dwell."
\f2\i0  The fullness dwelling in the Son is 
\f0\b the Father's pleasure
\f2\b0  \'97 granted by the Father.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 What Col 1:13-20 establishes:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 - The Son is the 
\f0\b Image
\f2\b0  of the invisible Father.\
- The Son is 
\f0\b firstborn over all creation
\f2\b0  (preeminent).\
- 
\f0\b All things were created in, through, and for Him.
\f2\b0 \
- He is 
\f0\b before all things
\f2\b0 .\
- 
\f0\b All things hold together in Him.
\f2\b0 \
- 
\f0\b The Father's fullness dwells in Him
\f2\b0  by the Father's pleasure.\
This passage, taken at face value, presents the Son as the agent of original creation \'97 which seems to stand in tension with Isaiah 44:24's 
\f1\i "I stretched out the heavens 
\f3\b alone
\f1\b0 ."
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Reconciliation honestly considered:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 The reconciliation must lie in this: 
\f0\b the Son acts as the Father's own agent
\f2\b0 , so that what the Father does 
\f1\i through
\f2\i0  the Son is still the Father's own act, done 
\f1\i alone
\f2\i0  in the sense that no other Deity participates. The Son is not "another God"; He is the Father's own Image, through whom the Father works. The Father's 
\f0\b Word
\f2\b0  (Ps 33:6) creates \'97 yet the Word is the Father's own, not a rival.\
This reading honors both Isa 44:24 and Col 1:16. The Father stretches out the heavens alone \'97 
\f1\i through His own Image, His own Firstborn, His own Word.
\f2\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 WITNESS C: Hebrews 1:1-3\
\pard\pardeftab720\li831\fi-832\sa200\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs33\fsmilli16667 \cf0 At sundry times and in divers manners God spake in the old time to our fathers by the Prophets: in these last days he hath spoken unto us by his Son, Whom he hath made heir of all things, by whom also he made the worlds, Who being the brightness of the glory, and the engraved form of his person, and bearing up all things by his mighty word, hath by himself purged our sins, and sitteth at the right hand of the Majesty in the highest places, \'97 
\f0\b Hebrews 1:1-3, 1599 Geneva
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Observations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 (1)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u8001  \u920 \u949 \u8056 \u962 ... \u7952 \u955 \u940 \u955 \u951 \u963 \u949 \u957  \u7969 \u956 \u8150 \u957  \u7952 \u957  \u933 \u7985 \u8183 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "God has spoken to us in [a/the] Son."
\f2\i0  The Father is the speaker; the Son is the medium/locus of His final speech.\

\f0\b (2)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u8003 \u957  \u7956 \u952 \u951 \u954 \u949 \u957  \u954 \u955 \u951 \u961 \u959 \u957 \u972 \u956 \u959 \u957  \u960 \u940 \u957 \u964 \u969 \u957 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "whom He appointed heir of all things."
\f2\i0  The Son is 
\f0\b appointed
\f2\b0  by the Father as heir. This is given language \'97 the Son receives by the Father's appointment.\

\f0\b (3)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u948 \u953 \u8125  \u959 \u8023  \u954 \u945 \u8054  \u7952 \u960 \u959 \u943 \u951 \u963 \u949 \u957  \u964 \u959 \u8058 \u962  \u945 \u7984 \u8182 \u957 \u945 \u962 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "through whom He also made the ages/worlds."
\f2\i0  The Father made the ages 
\f0\b through
\f2\b0  the Son. Same construction as 1 Cor 8:6, Col 1:16, John 1:3.\

\f0\b (4)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u7936 \u960 \u945 \u973 \u947 \u945 \u963 \u956 \u945  \u964 \u8134 \u962  \u948 \u972 \u958 \u951 \u962 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "radiance of [the Father's] glory."
\f2\i0  The Son is the 
\f1\i radiance
\f2\i0  \'97 what proceeds from and reveals the Father's glory. Light from light, so to speak.\

\f0\b (5)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u967 \u945 \u961 \u945 \u954 \u964 \u8052 \u961  \u964 \u8134 \u962  \u8017 \u960 \u959 \u963 \u964 \u940 \u963 \u949 \u969 \u962  \u945 \u8016 \u964 \u959 \u8166 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "the exact stamp/impression of His being."
\f2\i0  
\f0\b \uc0\u967 \u945 \u961 \u945 \u954 \u964 \u942 \u961 
\f2\b0  = the impression made by a die or seal. The Son bears the exact impression of the Father's reality.\

