\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Observations\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 (1)
\f2\b0  Jesus' own dying cry from the cross is 
\f0\b a direct quotation of Psalm 21:1 LXX (22:1 MT).
\f2\b0  This is the strongest possible witness: 
\f0\b Jesus Himself identifies His suffering with this Psalm.
\f2\b0  He does not merely quote a phrase from Scripture; He recites the opening line, claiming the entire Psalm as His own.\

\f0\b (2)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u8009  \u920 \u949 \u972 \u962  \u956 \u959 \u965 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "My God."
\f2\i0  The Christ on the cross addresses the Father as 
\f0\b My God.
\f2\b0  This is consistent with John 20:17 \'97 
\f1\i "I ascend to My Father and your Father, and My God and your God."
\f2\i0  The Son has a God; that God is the Father. 
\f0\b Even at the climax of His suffering, the Son's monotheistic confession is preserved.
\f2\b0 \

\f0\b (3)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u7952 \u947 \u954 \u945 \u964 \u941 \u955 \u953 \u960 \u941 \u962  \u956 \u949 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "You have forsaken Me."
\f2\i0  The Father forsakes the Son in this moment. This is a real abandonment \'97 not a feigned one. The Son experiences true forsakenness while bearing sin.\

\f0\b (4)
\f2\b0  Note the LXX reads 
\f0\b \uc0\u959 \u7985  \u955 \u972 \u947 \u959 \u953  \u964 \u8182 \u957  \u960 \u945 \u961 \u945 \u960 \u964 \u969 \u956 \u940 \u964 \u969 \u957  \u956 \u959 \u965 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "the words of my transgressions"
\f2\i0  \'97 where the Hebrew has 
\f1\i "the words of my roaring."
\f2\i0  The LXX makes the "transgressions" language explicit. Yet \'97 the Son had no transgressions of His own (Heb 4:15; 1 Pet 2:22). The "transgressions" must be the sins 
\f0\b He bore on behalf of others
\f2\b0  (Isa 53:6, 11-12), which we shall examine shortly.\
