\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Observations of significant weight\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 (1)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u946 \u945 \u963 \u953 \u955 \u949 \u8058 \u962  \u948 \u953 \u954 \u945 \u953 \u959 \u963 \u973 \u957 \u951 \u962  / \u946 \u945 \u963 \u953 \u955 \u949 \u8058 \u962  \u949 \u7984 \u961 \u942 \u957 \u951 \u962 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "King of Righteousness / King of Peace."
\f2\i0  These titles, drawn from the Hebrew etymology, foreshadow the dual prophecies of Christ as the Righteous One who brings peace.\

\f0\b (2)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u7936 \u960 \u940 \u964 \u969 \u961 , \u7936 \u956 \u942 \u964 \u969 \u961 , \u7936 \u947 \u949 \u957 \u949 \u945 \u955 \u972 \u947 \u951 \u964 \u959 \u962 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "without father, without mother, without genealogy."
\f2\i0  The author of Hebrews uses Melchizedek's 
\f1\i narrative silence
\f2\i0  about his lineage as a typological device. Melchizedek appears and disappears without genealogical context \'97 making him a fitting 
\f1\i type
\f2\i0  of an eternal Priest.\

\f0\b (3)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u7936 \u966 \u969 \u956 \u959 \u953 \u969 \u956 \u941 \u957 \u959 \u962  \u948 \u8050  \u964 \u8183  \u933 \u7985 \u8183  \u964 \u959 \u8166  \u920 \u949 \u959 \u8166 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "having been made like unto the Son of God."
\f2\i0  This is critical. 
\f0\b Melchizedek is made like the Son
\f2\b0  \'97 not the reverse. 
\f0\b The Son is the original; Melchizedek is the type.
\f2\b0  This confirms the Son's pre-existence and priority \'97 He is the reality of which Melchizedek is the foreshadow.\

\f0\b (4)
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u956 \u941 \u957 \u949 \u953  \u7985 \u949 \u961 \u949 \u8058 \u962  \u949 \u7984 \u962  \u964 \u8056  \u948 \u953 \u951 \u957 \u949 \u954 \u941 \u962 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "He continues a Priest perpetually."
\f2\i0  Of Melchizedek as narrative figure (and ultimately of the Son whom he prefigures).\
