\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Observation of considerable importance\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 Note the textual difference:
\f2\b0 \
- 
\f0\b LXX:
\f2\b0  
\f0\b \uc0\u963 \u8182 \u956 \u945  \u948 \u8050  \u954 \u945 \u964 \u951 \u961 \u964 \u943 \u963 \u969  \u956 \u959 \u953 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "a body You prepared for Me."
\f2\i0 \
- 
\f0\b Hebrew (followed by the 1599 Geneva):
\f2\b0  
\f1\i "mine ears hast thou prepared"
\f2\i0  (or "digged/opened").\

\f0\b The author of Hebrews uses the LXX reading
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f0\b \uc0\u963 \u8182 \u956 \u945  \u948 \u8050  \u954 \u945 \u964 \u951 \u961 \u964 \u943 \u963 \u969  \u956 \u959 \u953 
\f2\b0  \'97 
\f1\i "a body You prepared for Me."
\f2\i0  Per our maxim #1 and our commitment to the LXX, this is the God-breathed reading the Apostle uses.\
This text is striking: it is presented as 
\f0\b the Son speaking
\f2\b0  as He 
\f0\b enters the world
\f2\b0  (
\f0\b \uc0\u949 \u7984 \u963 \u949 \u961 \u967 \u972 \u956 \u949 \u957 \u959 \u962  \u949 \u7984 \u962  \u964 \u8056 \u957  \u954 \u972 \u963 \u956 \u959 \u957 
\f2\b0 ). The Son, on the eve of His incarnation, says to the Father: 
\f1\i "You did not want sacrifices and offerings \'97 but a body You prepared for Me. Behold, I come to do Your will, O God."
\f2\i0 \

\f0\b The Son speaks of His own coming into the world as His act of obedience to the Father's will.
\f2\b0  The Father prepared the body; the Son took it; the Son's purpose was to do the Father's will. This is the highest expression of the Father-Source / Son-Sent pattern, applied to the Incarnation itself.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
