\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 Two Critical Doctrinal Points Preserved\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\fs36 \cf0 (1) The Father remains the One God throughout the Son's return.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 - The 
\f0\b Ancient of Days
\f2\b0  (the Father) gives the Son the kingdom (Dan 7:14).\
- The 
\f0\b kingdom was prepared by the Father
\f2\b0  from the foundation of the world (Matt 25:34).\
- 
\f0\b Only the Father
\f2\b0  knows the day and hour (Matt 24:36; Mark 13:32; Acts 1:7).\
- The blessed are blessed 
\f0\b of the Father
\f2\b0  (Matt 25:34).\
- The Day of the LORD = the Day of the Lord Jesus, because the Son comes in the Father's Name with the Father's authority.\
- The consummation: 
\f0\b "one LORD, one Name"
\f2\b0  (Zech 14:9) \'97 the unified worship of the One God.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa360\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 (2) The Son acts always in the Father's Name and by the Father's authority.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 - He receives the kingdom from the Ancient of Days.\
- He returns at the Father's appointed time (which the Father alone knows).\
- He executes judgment by the authority 
\f0\b given
\f2\b0  to Him (John 5:22, 27).\
- His coming fulfills 
\f1\i both
\f2\i0  OT prophecies of the LORD's coming 
\f1\i and
\f2\i0  the Son of Man's coming \'97 because He is the LORD's Sent One, bearing the LORD's Name (Ex 23:21).\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 What This Means for Our Study\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 The Son's coming return is not the return of a second God or rival Deity. It is the visible, glorious appearing of the Father's Beloved Son, His Anointed Christ, His exalted Man, His high Priest forever \'97 coming to:\
- Vindicate His persecuted people.\
- Judge His enemies.\
- Establish the Father's universal Kingdom.\
- Be glorified in His saints.\
- Hand all things over to the Father (1 Cor 15:24-28, which we will examine in (C)).\
The pattern is unbroken from Genesis to the second coming: 
\f0\b the Father is the Source, the Son is the Sent One, and all things move toward the glory of God the Father.
\f2\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa331\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs40 \cf0 What Remains\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs34\fsmilli17333 \cf0 We have now completed (A) and (B). What remains:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa346\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 (C) The Son's final relation to the Father in the consummation
\f2\b0  \'97 1 Cor 15:24-28 \'97 perhaps the single most theologically decisive passage in all the NT on the Father-Son order at the end of all things. The Son delivers the Kingdom to the Father; the Son Himself is subjected to the Father; 
\f0\b God is all in all.
\f2\b0  This passage requires the utmost careful examination.\

\f0\b (D) The Son's titles and self-identifications in the Apocalypse
\f2\b0  \'97 the I AM sayings of Revelation, the Lamb on the Throne, Alpha and Omega designations, and how the Apocalypse weaves all our threads into the final tapestry.\
